cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
title: "fabric: the multiplayer-fabric repo manifest"
abstract: >
  A repo manifest checking out 53 projects across 5 GitHub organisations as one
  hexagon, whose numbered directories are the six positions RFD 0111 names, plus a
  packing of them and the record kept about the whole. Every project states its own
  remote and revision, so one that omits either fails at init rather than inheriting
  a default nobody chose. This repository holds default.xml and nothing else: the
  conventions, the doc gates, the delivery ledger and the logbook are a project on
  the manifest's own zero side, infrastructure-logbook, because a manifest is read
  by a tool on every sync and a record is rewritten as the work moves.
authors:
  - family-names: Lee
    given-names: "K. S. Ernest (iFire)"
    email: ernest.lee@chibifire.com
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0003-4570-1436"
version: 0.1.0-dev.1
date-released: "2026-08-16"
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/v-sekai-multiplayer-fabric/fabric"
keywords:
  - repo-manifest
  - monorepo
  - hexagonal-architecture
references:

  # ── The tool this manifest is written for ────────────────────────────────────
  # One reference, because this repository is one file plus the prose about it. The
  # tools the record is built on (Beancount, OpenUSD), the papers its estimation
  # method comes from, and the projects its ledger books cite all moved with the
  # work, to infrastructure-logbook's own CITATION.cff.
  - type: software
    title: "repo: a tool built on top of Git for managing many repositories"
    authors:
      - name: "The Android Open Source Project"
    year: 2026
    url: "https://gerrit.googlesource.com/git-repo"
    notes: >
      Reads default.xml and checks out every project. The manifest's path
      attribute, including the path="." self-entry that puts this repository at
      the workspace root, is repo's own behaviour.
